# __PROJECT__

C++__CPP_STANDARD__ project using MSVC x64, CMake 3.28+ and Ninja, with a
`libs/core` library target, a thin `app` executable, and Catch2 tests.
Use VS Code, Neovim or Visual Studio. Install the compiler/Windows SDK and CMake/Ninja;
the build helper discovers an existing C++ installation and does not install tools.

```powershell
./scripts/Build.ps1 -Test
./build/dev/bin/__PROJECT__.exe
./scripts/Build.ps1 -Configuration Release
```

Or run `./scripts/Enter-DevShell.ps1`, then use the CMake `dev`/`release` presets directly.
An editor launched from that shell inherits the environment. Configure editor diagnostics
from `build/dev/compile_commands.json` instead of maintaining a second set of flags.

The initial targets use the dynamic MSVC CRT, exceptions, and warnings as errors for owned
code. Record the actual compiler and SDK versions after building. C++23 may map to
`/std:c++latest` in a particular CMake/MSVC pairing; inspect the actual compiler command.
Choose and test runtime/deployment settings when the application gains consumers.

Keep reusable logic in `libs/core`; extract a second library target once a second consumer
actually appears. The sample `core::greeting()` function and its Catch2 case exist only to
prove the scaffold builds and tests end to end — replace them with real logic.
`./scripts/Build.ps1 -Test` runs CTest. `-Fresh` reconfigures after a toolchain change. Use
separate build directories/presets for different architectures or generators. Formatting/
analysis files are included only when existing configurations were supplied to the generator.
